WEBVTT

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:03.761
Every enterprise has architecture standards.

00:00:04.241 --> 00:00:07.601
Very few can check every design against them.

00:00:07.801 --> 00:00:12.481
This is Architecture Compliance Review: an AI-assisted workspace that

00:00:12.721 --> 00:00:15.761
reviews system designs against your own standards,

00:00:15.841 --> 00:00:19.681
for the systems you run in the cloud and in your own datacenters.

00:00:20.483 --> 00:00:25.070
Design reviews are where resilience and security are won or lost.

00:00:25.096 --> 00:00:28.030
But the standards live in long policy documents,

00:00:28.056 --> 00:00:31.963
the designs arrive as hundred-page PDFs and slide decks,

00:00:32.003 --> 00:00:34.683
and every review is done by hand.

00:00:34.723 --> 00:00:39.283
It takes weeks, and two reviewers rarely reach the same verdict.

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:45.399
Cloud providers now offer self-service checks against their own frameworks.

00:00:45.439 --> 00:00:47.919
But enterprise estates are hybrid.

00:00:47.999 --> 00:00:52.959
On-premises systems, internal standards, and house diagram notations sit

00:00:53.039 --> 00:00:58.799
outside those tools, and that is where many of the most critical systems still run.

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:04.600
Architecture Compliance Review closes that gap in four steps.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:08.320
Turn your policy documents into a governed rule library.

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:11.120
Teach it your own diagram notation.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.640
Upload a design, from the cloud or from your own datacenter.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:20.640
Then validate it, with every verdict cited back to the page it came from.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.480
People stay in control at every step.

00:01:24.803 --> 00:01:27.803
Teams sign in with their corporate identity.

00:01:27.843 --> 00:01:32.643
With single sign-on through SAML, directory groups map straight to roles

00:01:32.723 --> 00:01:34.163
in the workspace.

00:01:34.884 --> 00:01:39.484
The dashboard is the portfolio view: how many designs pass,

00:01:39.524 --> 00:01:43.524
where the critical risk sits, and a priority queue of the reviews that

00:01:43.604 --> 00:01:45.444
need attention first.

00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:48.167
Access is role-based.

00:01:48.247 --> 00:01:50.487
Admins manage the workspace.

00:01:50.567 --> 00:01:53.087
Curators own the rule library.

00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:56.087
Validators submit and review designs.

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:58.767
Viewers can read and ask questions.

00:01:58.807 --> 00:02:04.487
And every change to rules, documents and validations is written to an audit trail.

00:02:05.209 --> 00:02:08.489
Step one: turn policy into rules.

00:02:09.891 --> 00:02:13.531
A curator uploads a standards document: here,

00:02:13.571 --> 00:02:17.371
AnyCompany Bank's standard for on-premises infrastructure.

00:02:17.411 --> 00:02:20.104
They tell the extractor what it is looking at:

00:02:20.158 --> 00:02:23.451
the tiers it covers, that its rules sit in tables,

00:02:23.491 --> 00:02:27.331
and that some rules are alternatives or exceptions to others.

00:02:28.212 --> 00:02:33.452
Extraction runs in the background, and every step streams to a live log:

00:02:33.492 --> 00:02:38.892
each page read, each rule found, and each decision the agent makes.

00:02:38.932 --> 00:02:40.932
Nothing is a black box.

00:02:47.655 --> 00:02:51.415
Extracted rules are proposals, not policy.

00:02:51.495 --> 00:02:55.015
Each one carries the exact quote and page it came from,

00:02:55.095 --> 00:02:59.095
so a curator can check it against the source in a single click.

00:02:59.898 --> 00:03:03.658
Full-screen review puts the document beside the rules.

00:03:03.738 --> 00:03:07.058
The extractor also captured how the rules relate:

00:03:07.098 --> 00:03:10.898
a founding requirement, a substitution that can satisfy it,

00:03:10.938 --> 00:03:14.298
and an exception that waives it for a lower tier.

00:03:16.864 --> 00:03:20.584
When the curator is satisfied, they approve the batch.

00:03:20.624 --> 00:03:24.384
Only approved rules are ever used in a validation.

00:03:26.607 --> 00:03:31.167
The library now holds both standards, filterable by hosting scope,

00:03:31.247 --> 00:03:34.007
tier, layer and rule type.

00:03:34.047 --> 00:03:38.567
Founding rules carry their alternatives and exceptions as a decision tree,

00:03:38.607 --> 00:03:43.007
which the validator applies before it ever calls a requirement failed.

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:47.930
References hold the context a reviewer needs but cannot test,

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:51.210
such as tier definitions and regulatory mappings.

00:03:51.250 --> 00:03:53.970
They travel with the rules they relate to.

00:03:54.692 --> 00:03:58.452
And Curate with AI scans the library for gaps,

00:03:58.532 --> 00:04:03.092
proposing new rules or edits for a curator to accept or reject.

00:04:03.895 --> 00:04:06.935
Each proposal explains the gap it would close,

00:04:07.015 --> 00:04:10.335
and nothing changes until a curator decides.

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:14.895
This one fills a real gap, so it is approved into the library.

00:04:14.935 --> 00:04:18.375
This one is not needed, so it is rejected.

00:04:21.098 --> 00:04:24.218
Step two: teach it your notation.

00:04:25.619 --> 00:04:30.899
On-premises diagrams are drawn in an organization's own vocabulary.

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:35.699
At AnyCompany Bank, an orange hexagon is a core banking node,

00:04:35.779 --> 00:04:38.939
a purple pentagon is a payment-signing module,

00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:42.619
and a page-shaped symbol is a datacenter site.

00:04:42.659 --> 00:04:47.299
A model reading the drawing cold sees only shapes, and guesses.

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:51.101
So the curator loads the bank's notation table,

00:04:51.141 --> 00:04:55.261
one row per symbol, then attaches the symbol images.

00:04:55.301 --> 00:04:59.701
The AI pairs each image with its row, and checks the picture against the

00:04:59.861 --> 00:05:00.981
written meaning.

00:05:02.205 --> 00:05:05.485
Anything it doubts is flagged before approval.

00:05:05.565 --> 00:05:11.045
Here it questions whether a page-shaped symbol can really mean a datacenter site.

00:05:11.085 --> 00:05:15.045
In this notation it does, so the curator keeps it.

00:05:15.085 --> 00:05:19.805
An ambiguous placeholder, and a test row with no image, are rejected.

00:05:20.526 --> 00:05:22.526
The rest are approved.

00:05:22.686 --> 00:05:27.646
Only approved symbols are ever used when the validator reads a diagram.

00:05:30.951 --> 00:05:36.391
Step three: bring a design, from your own datacenter or the cloud.

00:05:37.793 --> 00:05:42.006
A validator uploads the design for the Retail Payments Platform,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:45.593
a Tier 1 system running in two owned datacenters,

00:05:45.633 --> 00:05:48.553
and sets its hosting scope to on-premises,

00:05:48.593 --> 00:05:52.513
so only the rules that apply to owned infrastructure are used.

00:05:56.237 --> 00:05:59.837
Within seconds the design is parsed into sections,

00:06:00.157 --> 00:06:04.317
recognized as Tier 1 from its own text, and both diagrams,

00:06:04.437 --> 00:06:08.477
drawn in the bank's notation, are extracted for visual analysis.

00:06:10.282 --> 00:06:13.802
Step four: validate, with evidence.

00:06:15.204 --> 00:06:18.124
A validator can run every applicable rule,

00:06:18.164 --> 00:06:22.684
pick rules by hand, or describe the review in plain language.

00:06:22.724 --> 00:06:26.684
Here they ask for site resilience across both datacenters,

00:06:26.724 --> 00:06:31.524
perimeter firewalls, payment-signing modules and failover testing.

00:06:33.048 --> 00:06:37.528
The AI picks the twenty library rules most relevant to that brief,

00:06:37.608 --> 00:06:42.048
from both standards, and can propose new rules for this run alone.

00:06:42.088 --> 00:06:45.608
A curator can save any of them to the library later.

00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:53.933
A verdict in minutes, not weeks: twenty-one percent and not compliant,

00:06:54.013 --> 00:06:58.053
with seventeen failures, four passes, and one warning.

00:06:58.093 --> 00:07:02.413
It caught what the text admits, such as quarterly failover tests where

00:07:02.493 --> 00:07:06.733
Tier 1 needs them monthly, and a twenty-two minute recovery against a

00:07:06.773 --> 00:07:08.493
fifteen minute target.

00:07:09.215 --> 00:07:12.415
And this is where the icon legend pays off.

00:07:12.495 --> 00:07:15.255
Reading the drawing in the bank's own notation,

00:07:15.295 --> 00:07:19.575
the validator found a single perimeter firewall serving both sites,

00:07:19.615 --> 00:07:24.535
where each site needs its own, a gap that appears only in the drawing.

00:07:24.575 --> 00:07:28.148
It also confirmed one payment-signing module per site,

00:07:28.202 --> 00:07:30.095
where a pair is required.

00:07:30.817 --> 00:07:35.697
Every finding quotes its evidence, cites the page, and recommends a fix.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.180
The full model trace is one click away, with the reasoning,

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:45.620
the prompt and the response, for anyone who needs to audit how a verdict was reached.

00:07:46.422 --> 00:07:50.022
And a reviewer can question any finding directly.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:09.930
Results export to PDF and CSV for the architecture review board.

00:08:12.734 --> 00:08:17.774
Across the whole portfolio, anyone can ask questions in plain language.

00:08:17.854 --> 00:08:22.654
Answers are grounded in the documents, and every claim cites its source.

00:08:24.294 --> 00:08:27.574
Every passage it reads is labelled with its source,

00:08:27.654 --> 00:08:30.774
so the answer quotes this design's own risk table,

00:08:30.854 --> 00:08:35.334
on page two, and maps each risk to the standard it breaches.

00:08:38.136 --> 00:08:44.056
It all runs in your own AWS account: ECS Fargate behind CloudFront,

00:08:44.136 --> 00:08:48.616
Amazon Bedrock for every model call, a Bedrock guardrail that redacts

00:08:48.696 --> 00:08:52.189
sensitive data, customer-managed encryption,

00:08:52.323 --> 00:08:55.136
CloudTrail alongside an application audit trail,

00:08:55.176 --> 00:08:57.056
and end-to-end tracing.

00:08:57.096 --> 00:08:59.736
Your documents never leave your account.

00:09:00.966 --> 00:09:04.966
The result: consistent reviews against your own standards,

00:09:05.046 --> 00:09:09.606
for cloud and on-premises systems alike, with people in control and

00:09:09.686 --> 00:09:12.086
evidence behind every decision.

00:09:13.311 --> 00:09:15.631
Architecture Compliance Review.

00:09:15.711 --> 00:09:18.991
Open source, and ready to deploy in your account.

